rd's label?). But token_labels are integers; could be per token.\n\nTry to deduce expected output from fixtures? Not given explicitly. But we can infer from typical NLP tasks: e.g., extract tokens for certain classes, assign labels. Since there is only one label of 1 in first case, maybe only tokens from word with label 1 are kept. In case0: word_labels[0]=1, tokens [0,1,2]; kept_indices might be [0,1,2], token_labels [1,1,1]? Or [1]? But token_labels length should match kept_indices. In case1: word_labels[1]=1, tokens [2,3]; kept_indices [2,3], token_labels [1,1]. In case2: word_labels[2]=1 and word_labels[3]=1, tokens [6,7] and [8,9]; kept_indices [6,7,8,9], token_labels [1,1,1,1]? But could also be 0 and 1? Since word_labels are 1, they are positive. Or token_labels could be the word_label value? That would be all 1s for kept tokens. But then token_labels would be all 1. Possibly token_labels is the label for each token, which could be the word's label. That seems plausible.\n\nBut need to ensure that token_labels length matches kept_indices. Also note token_count is given but not used maybe.\n\nAlternative: maybe need to assign label 0 to tokens from words with label 0? But then kept_indices would be all tokens? That would be too many. The output schema has kept_indices and token_labels; likely kept_indices are subset. So we select tokens from words with label 1.\n\nCheck case2: two words with label 1. So kept tokens: [6,7,8,9]. That makes sense.\n\nThus algorithm: iterate over word_token_indices and word_labels. For each word, if word_label == 1, extend kept_indices with token indices, and extend token_labels with [word_label]*len(token_indices). Else skip.\n\nBut also consider possibility that token_labels might be different per token? Not in this simple scenario.\n\nAlternatively maybe we need to keep all tokens but assign labels per token based on word labels? Then kept_indices would be all token indices, and token_labels would be per token. But then kept_indices would be [0..token_count-1]. That would be trivial but not matching output? The output schema doesn't require kept_indices to be a subset, it just requires an array. But from task name \"NLP-LL2-01\" maybe it's about sequence labeling and keeping only certain spans? The word \"kept_indices\" suggests filtering.\n\nLet's verify with possible hidden test cases. The fixture is public but not showing expected output. The instruction: \"The evaluator invokes solve(case) once for each fixture row.\" So we need to produce correct output for all fixture rows. Since we have only public ones, we need to ensure logic matches.\n\nMaybe there is a hidden requirement: \"token_labels\" could be the label of the word that each token belongs to, but for tokens from words with label 0, they are also included? Then kept_indices would be all tokens. But then why call \"kept\"? Could be \"kept_indices\" meaning indices of tokens that are kept (maybe after some truncation or selection). The word \"kept\" suggests selection.\n\nAlso note token_count is given, but we can compute total tokens from word_token_indices. Not needed.\n\nI think the simplest interpretation: Keep all tokens that belong to words whose label is 1. Return kept_indices (list of token indices) and token_labels (list of labels for those tokens, presumably the word label, which is 1). That yields:\n\nCase0: kept_indices=[0,1,2], token_labels=[1,1,1]\nCase1: kept_indices=[2,3], token_labels=[1,1]\nCase2: kept_indices=[6,7,8,9], token_labels=[1,1,1,1]\n\nThat seems plausible.\n\nBut why token_labels would be all 1? Could also be the actual word label value, which is 1 for positive, 0 for negative? But in case2, word_labels[2]=1, word_labels[3]=1, so both 1. So still 1.\n\nIf there were words with label 1 and label 0? But we only keep label 1. So token_labels always 1.\n\nMaybe we also need to include tokens from words with label 0? No.\n\nAnother possibility: The task might be to perform \"token labeling\" where each